#
# NVIDIA Infra Controller -- discovery OS (Scout) cloud-init
# =========================================================
#
# This document is generated by the PXE service from the files a site places in
# its Scout snippet directory. Editing it achieves nothing; change the snippet
# files. Every servable file found there is listed below in filename order, and
# cloud-init fetches and applies each in turn. Nothing here parses, merges, or
# validates their contents. Each file must use a user-data format cloud-init
# recognizes; a cloud-config file starts with #cloud-config. No
# product-specific dialect is required.
#
# COMPOSING SNIPPETS.
# Each cloud-config snippet is a separate cloud-config document, and
# cloud-init's default merge strategy for lists is REPLACE, not append. Two
# cloud-config snippets that both define runcmd -- or write_files, or packages
# -- therefore do not combine: the later document's list replaces the earlier
# one's, and the earlier snippet's entries never run. Nothing reports this. The
# boot still completes cleanly, cloud-init still exits zero, and the only
# symptom is that one of your snippets appears to have done nothing. Declare
# merge_how in each cloud-config snippet to make them additive:
#
#     merge_how: 'dict(recurse_array,no_replace)+list(append)'
#
# This is cloud-init's own behaviour, not something added here, and it applies
# to any set of cloud-config documents merged together. A single cloud-config
# snippet needs none of it and works unmodified. Cloud-config snippets that use
# different top-level keys also compose without it; only a shared key collides.
# Other user-data formats, such as scripts, do not use this merge policy.
#
# TIME BOUND.
# Before registering the machine, Scout runs cloud-init status --wait --long.
# Normally all snippets have finished, successfully or with errors, before
# Scout proceeds. This wait has a 600-second backstop. If the status command has
# not returned by then, Scout continues with registration; timing out that
# command does not stop cloud-final.service, so a snippet may still be running.
#
# Independently, cloud-init's own systemd units bound each stage. A final-stage
# module such as runcmd runs under cloud-final.service and is terminated if it
# exceeds that unit's TimeoutStartSec. Unfinished work is lost and is not
# retried later in the same boot. To see the bound actually in force at this
# site, on any host running the discovery OS:
#
#     systemctl show -p TimeoutStartUSec cloud-final.service
#
# That unit value is deliberately not restated here because it is controlled by
# the Scout image and may change independently of this template.
#
# ############################################################################
# ###                                                                      ###
# ###     !!!   DO NOT REBOOT FROM A SNIPPET.  EVER.   !!!                 ###
# ###                                                                      ###
# ############################################################################
# Scout runs entirely from a RAMdisk, so a reboot is not a way to settle
# changes -- it is a full re-PXE that discards the overlay, re-downloads the
# rootfs, and runs these same snippets again from scratch. A snippet that
# reboots therefore does not cost one reboot; it costs an unbounded reboot loop
# across every machine in discovery at this site, because the machine never
# reaches the point of registering and so keeps being served Scout. Nothing
# breaks that cycle on its own. Anything needing a reboot to take effect --
# kernel parameters, module changes, firmware activation -- cannot be delivered
# this way and belongs in the image or in a lifecycle state that owns a reboot.
#
# SNIPPETS RE-RUN.
# Reboots do happen legitimately, for image updates and lifecycle transitions,
# and these snippets run again each time on a clean rootfs. Local state is
# therefore always fresh and needs no first-run guarding, but any effect
# reaching outside the rootfs must be idempotent: calling an API, mutating BMC
# or firmware state, writing to persistent disk, or consuming a license seat
# will happen more than once in a machine's life.
#
# NO SECRETS.
# These files are served from /public, which is unauthenticated by necessity --
# its clients are machines that hold no credentials yet -- so anything placed in
# a snippet is readable by anything that can reach this service. A snippet
# needing privileged material must fetch it at runtime from an authenticated
# source rather than embed it.
#
{% if snippet_urls | length > 0 %}{% for url in snippet_urls %}{{ url }}
{% endfor %}{% else %}# No snippets are configured at this site. This is a deliberate no-op document
# rather than an empty one, so the unconfigured path stays deterministic.
{}
{% endif %}
